{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The claim asserts that the cross-city variation in the nocturnal UHI is 'best explained by differences in urban sky view factor.' But the mechanism offered conflates sky view factor's local effect (slowing radiative cooling at street level) with a cross-city explanatory variable, and then the countercondition concedes that 'if a city with dense urban geometry still exhibits a weaker nocturnal UHI due to high ventilation or coastal winds, sky view factor alone is insufficient.' That concession is not hypothetical: it is the empirically dominant cross-city modulator. Oke's review of observed nocturnal UHI maxima against population shows the cross-city magnitude scales primarily with city size/population (an anthropogenic heat and total-emitting-area proxy), not with geometric sky view factor. Sky view factor varies block-to-block within a single city and cannot, by itself, account for why a large hot/arid metropolis has a much bigger nocturnal UHI than a small well-ventilated dense city. Because the problem asks for the single mechanism that best explains the cross-city gap, naming sky view factor as that single mechanism is a genuine fault: it explains within-city spatial variation in cooling rate, not the observed between-city variation in the nighttime temperature gap.","premise":"The problem asks for the single mechanism that best explains why the nocturnal UHI gap is larger in some cities than in others; the target answers 'sky view factor' as that single mechanism.","premise_evidence":null,"counterexample":null}]}